window.docContents[187].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Pull":{"contents":"Once A and B are coupled, what bears the total mass and the oscillation frequency? Open the L1 black boxes into bodies A and B and springs kA, kC, and kB; kC is the new coupling spring. Each wall-spring oscillator supplies its body's mass and wall-spring stiffness, and the coupling spring makes the oscillators interact, so L1's no-interaction assumption falls. Mass is borne by the bodies and stiffness by the springs; the L1 oscillator-indexed ω² = k/m does not survive coupling. The normal-mode angular frequency belongs to the pair whole, which has its own sort. Following L1, one PairId name is one system: records under it are its states at different times, configurations, or rival estimates; comparing their shared-bearer quantities is the point. Different names denote distinct systems, so P and Q remain distinct even when their parameters match. Each spring has a signed extension, negative when compressed and positive when stretched; two configurations of pair P can compare their coupling-spring extensions with a typed displacement difference.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.3. Pull","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Pull"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Pull":{"contents":"The pair now has two bodies, a coupling spring, and multiple records. What must hold, for which systems or objects? A requirement states its quantification scope, writable because the earlier levels named the pair sort and its nominal particulars. One name is one system: records named P are states, configurations, or rival estimates of that same pair and can be compared; Q is a distinct pair. An object-scoped requirement on P governs every state or record of P, not only records used for a spot check. The full worked requirements example is examples/Tutorial/HarmonicOscillatorRequirements.lean; this lesson mirrors its machinery without importing that file.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.3. Pull","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Pull"}});